---
alwaysApply: true
---

{
"rules": [
{
"description": "NestJS best practices for clean, maintainable backend code",
"patterns": ["*.ts"],
"instructions": [
// Structure & Modularity
"Always follow NestJS modular architecture — group code by domain (modules, controllers, services, entities).",
"Each feature should have its own module that encapsulates its controller, service, and related providers.",
"Avoid putting unrelated logic in a single module or service.",

        // Code Clarity & Simplicity
        "Write clear, simple, and maintainable code — avoid overengineering or unnecessary abstractions.",
        "Prefer readability over cleverness.",
        "Apply SOLID and DRY principles — each class and function should have a single responsibility.",
        "Keep methods small and focused; return early to avoid deep nesting.",
        "Use meaningful, descriptive names for classes, methods, and variables.",

        // Dependency Injection
        "Use NestJS dependency injection properly — always inject dependencies via constructors.",
        "Do not manually instantiate classes using `new` inside providers or controllers.",
        "Register services and repositories in their module’s providers array.",

        // Controllers
        "Controllers should remain thin — handle only routing, validation, and response formatting.",
        "Move business logic to services or dedicated providers.",
        "Use DTOs for request validation and transformation.",
        "Use NestJS decorators like @Body, @Param, and @Query consistently.",

        // Services
        "Services should contain all business logic and call repositories or other services as needed.",
        "Keep services modular and reusable — avoid circular dependencies.",
        "Avoid writing long service methods; split complex logic into helper functions.",

        // Error Handling
        "Use NestJS built-in exceptions (HttpException and subclasses) for error handling.",
        "Do not return raw error objects — throw exceptions with meaningful messages and status codes.",
        "Centralize error handling with exception filters when appropriate.",

        // DTOs & Validation
        "Always define DTOs for data transfer and validation using class-validator and class-transformer.",
        "Keep DTOs simple — only include fields relevant to each operation (Create, Update, etc.).",
        "Validate all input data at the controller level before passing it to the service.",

        // Entities & Persistence
        "Keep entities clean and focused on database schema — avoid adding business logic inside entities.",
        "Use TypeORM or Prisma repositories for database access, not raw queries.",
        "Avoid mixing repository logic with service logic.",
        "Use migrations for schema changes — never modify tables manually.",

        // Configuration & Environment
        "Use NestJS ConfigModule to manage environment variables.",
        "Never hardcode credentials or secrets — always load from .env files.",
        "Use ConfigService for environment-based configurations.",

        // Error & Logging
        "Use NestJS Logger for consistent logging instead of console.log.",
        "Log important lifecycle events, errors, and warnings meaningfully.",
        "Avoid logging sensitive information (passwords, tokens, etc.).",

        // Testing
        "Write unit tests for controllers and services using Jest.",
        "Mock external dependencies and focus tests on behavior, not implementation details.",
        "Keep test setup minimal and use Nest's TestingModule for integration-style tests.",

        // API & Documentation
        "Use Swagger decorators (@ApiTags, @ApiProperty, etc.) for API documentation.",
        "Maintain consistent HTTP status codes and response structures.",
        "Always document expected responses, including error formats.",

        // Performance & Security
        "Use caching or pagination for endpoints that return large datasets.",
        "Sanitize and validate all inputs to prevent injection attacks.",
        "Enable CORS and Helmet for security best practices.",
        "Avoid synchronous blocking calls in request handlers.",

        // General
        "Adhere to consistent code formatting and naming conventions.",
        "Keep imports clean and organized — no unused imports or circular dependencies.",
        "Follow NestJS conventions strictly to maintain readability and consistency."
      ]
    }

]
}
